  "body": "According below spec, any html document created by using srcdoc will be rendered in no-quirks mode, no matter what the actual doctype is. \r\n\r\nHere is the spec:\r\n\r\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#the-initial-insertion-mode\r\n\r\nMajor browsers such as chrome currently compliant with it, however that causes confusions.\r\n\r\nFor example when devs are inpecting the dom tree, Chrome shows the doctype as 'loose', which make devs thinking the content will be rendered in quirks mode, and later found out browser actually renders it in standard mode.\r\n\r\nAlso, this forces devs to fallback to use the retiring `document.write()` in order to set the doctype and make it renders in quirks mode.\r\n\r\nHere are some tests I made to show the difference between `document.write(doctype)` and `srcdoc='<doctype html loose >'`:\r\n\r\nhttps://jsfiddle.net/brzhv2fg/\r\n\r\nHence I personally recommand either we retire quirks mode before retiring document.write(), or,\r\nmake iframe.srcdoc support quirks mode doctypes correctly.\r\n\r\nIn case you are curious about what is the use case for us to display quirk mode page in an iframe:\r\nwe are developing an co-browsing app that sync the web page to remote user so they can see what the controller has seen.\r\n\r\n",
